course: language
lesson: 12
title: Spelling traps: their, there, they're and friends
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some English words sound the same but are spelled differently and mean different things: they're (they are), their (belonging to them) and there (that place): "They're going to their house over there."
explain: The swap test catches most traps: if you can say "they are", write they're; if you can say "you are", write you're; if you can say "it is" or "it has", write it's; otherwise it is their, your or its.
explain: Little helpers live inside big words: definitely has "finite" in the middle (de-finite-ly), and there's "a rat" in sep-a-rat-e; "too" has one o too many, because it means extra.
explain: Some famous rules are weak: "i before e except after c" fails for science, weird, their, height and eight, so treat it as a hint, not a law.
explain: Even grown-ups who know the rules make typos when they type fast or when autocorrect "helps"; text speak like "c u l8r m8" is fine with mates, but school work wants full spellings.
fact: They're means they are; their means belonging to them; there means in that place, or is used in "there is".
fact: You're means you are; your means belonging to you.
fact: It's means it is or it has; its means belonging to it and, like his and hers, has no apostrophe.
fact: Two is the number 2; too means also or very; to goes before a verb or shows direction.
fact: Lose (said with a z sound) means to not have something any more; loose means not tight.
fact: "Would have" is the correct written form, not "would of".
fact: Definitely comes from "finite", meaning having an end: de-finite-ly.
fact: "I before e except after c" works for receive and believe, but science, weird, their, height, eight, seize and ancient break it; some UK teaching guides advise against teaching it as a rule.
fact: Text speak shortens words with letters and numbers that sound like them: c (see), u (you), l8r (later), m8 (mate).
fact: Weather is rain, sun and wind; whether means if, as in "I don't know whether to go".
fact: The correct spelling is separate; "there's a rat in separate" is a popular trick.
fact: A typo is a typing mistake; a mistake doesn't always mean someone doesn't know the rule.
fact: "Grammar police" is a jokey name for people who correct others' spelling; correcting people can feel rude, so do it kindly or not at all.
exercise: Mo writes "Their going to there house over they're." What is right? | They're going to their house over there | There going to they're house over their | Their going to they're house over there | They're going to there house over their
exercise: Which word means "belonging to them"? | their | there | they're | thier
exercise: Ella's cousin writes "Your so funny." What should it be? | You're so funny | Yore so funny | Your's so funny | You so funny're
exercise: Sam writes "The dog wagged it's tail." What's right? | its tail | it's tail | its' tail | it is tail
exercise: Which sentence is correct? | It's raining and the cat licked its paw | Its raining and the cat licked it's paw | It's raining and the cat licked it's paw | Its raining and the cat licked its paw
exercise: A shop sign says "Two good to miss! Prices to low!" What should it say? | Too good to miss! Prices too low! | To good to miss! Prices two low! | Two good to miss! Prices too low! | Too good too miss! Prices to low!
exercise: Zainab's tooth wobbles. Which word fits: "My tooth is ___"? | loose | lose | lost | loss
exercise: Which is the correct written form? | I would have gone | I would of gone | I would off gone | I would've went
exercise: Elliot types "definately". What helps him spell it? | Spot "finite" inside: de-finite-ly | Remember that it rhymes with "late" and add the letters | Spell it like it sounds | Use "i before e"
exercise: Kai says "i before e except after c" doesn't always work. Which word breaks it? | weird | believe | receive | piece
exercise: Tom texts "c u l8r m8". What does it mean? | See you later, mate | Come up later, mate | See you, latte made | Call you late, mate
exercise: Leila writes "I don't know weather to go." What should it be? | whether | wether | weather's | wheather
exercise: Rory writes "seperate". What trick helps? | There's a rat in separate | There's a pear in separate | Separate has two e's at the end | Separate sounds like "seper"
exercise: Dad texts "Bring you're coat" in a rush. What's going on? | A typo from typing fast; he knows the rule | He can't spell at all and never learned the rule | You're is the right form here | Autocorrect is always right
exercise: Ella replies "*you're" and her cousin says "ok grammar police". What's the wise way? | Correct friends kindly or not at all | Always correct everyone loudly | Never use the right spelling | Report them to a teacher
exercise: How many spelling mistakes in "Its to late, there all asleep"? | Three: Its, to, there | Two: to and there | One: Its | None
exercise: Fill both gaps: "___ coming to ___ party." | They're, our | Their, our | There, hour | They're, are
exercise: Which word breaks "i before e except after c" even though it has c? | science | receive | ceiling | receipt
test: Your mate goes "they're, their, there, same thing innit, sounds the same." What's the best reply? | They sound alike but mean different things, so writing needs the right one | He's right, any of them works because they sound the same | Only "there" is a real word | They're is American, their is British
test: A Gen Z cousin comments "ur drawing slaps" on your art. ("Slaps" means is brilliant.) If you wrote it in full in school English, what would it be? | Your drawing is brilliant | You're drawing is brilliant | Yore drawing is brilliant | Your drawing slaps you
test: What would happen if we spelled every word exactly as it sounds? | Their, there and they're would all look the same | Nothing, because they already look different from each other | We'd need more letters for "there" | Nobody could write "too"
test: A sign says "Dog's must be on there leads." How many mistakes? | Two: Dog's and there | One: there | Three: Dog's, must, there | None, it's correct
test: Your nan from Cornwall, proper old-school, says "spelling's just being born good at it, my lover." ("My lover" is a friendly greeting there.) What's true? | Tricks and patterns help anyone learn hard spellings | She's right, nobody can learn spelling | Only grown-ups can learn spelling | Spelling is just luck each time
test: Your teacher swears by "i before e except after c." Which word gives her a headache? | their | believe | receive | field
test: Which is right: "Don't ___ your keys, the ring is ___"? | lose, loose | loose, lose | lose, lose | loose, loose
test: Your dad, typing fast, writes "I'll pick u up at too." What did he mean? | At two o'clock | Also | Very much | Towards you
test: In a cartoon, a pirate's map says "The tresure is over they're." Fix the last words. | over there | over their | over thier | over they'r
test: Grandad says "I never know weather to wear a coat." Fix one word. | whether | wether | weathers | weather's
test: Your mate writes "definatly" and insists "it's lowkey impossible to spell." ("Lowkey" means kind of.) What's the tip? | Find "finite" inside definitely | Add more a's until it looks right | Spell it with an i before e | Just write "def"
exam: Identify the formulation consistent with the conventions of written English. | They're going to their house over there | Their going to there house over they're | There going to they're house over their | They're going to there house over their
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the form "it's"? | A contraction of "it is" or "it has" | The possessive form of "it" | A plural of the word "it" | An archaic spelling of "its"
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the rule "i before e except after c". | It admits numerous exceptions, such as "science", "weird" and "their" | It admits no exceptions whatsoever | It applies solely to words of Latin origin | It was abolished by statute
exam: Ascertain the correctly spelled form of the word meaning "with certainty". | definitely | definately | definatly | defiantly
